WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:07.087
Joshua Johnson: Going through life
with a willingness to ask more questions

00:00:07.087 --> 00:00:08.463
than you make statements?

00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:09.923
Liberating.

00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:12.926
Nothing that happens after the question
is your responsibility.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:16.972
Your responsibility is to have
the curiosity, the integrity,

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:18.515
the thoughtfulness,

00:00:18.557 --> 00:00:21.601
and the humility
to ask what's on your mind

00:00:21.643 --> 00:00:25.605
and not bite your tongue because,
well, it might be uncomfortable,

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:27.232
well, what are they going to say?

00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:28.525
Let that be their problem.

00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:31.152
[Intersections]

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.703
[Joshua Johnson, Journalist]

00:00:40.912 --> 00:00:43.373
[Blake Mycoskie, Social entrepreneur]

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:45.917
JJ: What if we shuffle the deck?

00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:47.127
BM: I'm fine with that.

00:00:47.168 --> 00:00:48.378
JJ: Alright.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.130
OK, deck duly shuffled.

00:00:50.171 --> 00:00:51.381
BM: OK, you go first now.

00:00:51.381 --> 00:00:53.341
JJ: Alright, so I can own what I've done?

00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:54.634
BM: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:00:54.676 --> 00:00:56.052
JJ: Alright, first question.

00:00:56.052 --> 00:01:00.015
Are we losing the ability
to have real conversations?

00:01:01.224 --> 00:01:02.392
BM: Hmm.

00:01:03.268 --> 00:01:04.686
I don't think so, actually.

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:08.607
I think that with everything
that's going on

00:01:08.648 --> 00:01:10.567
from a technical perspective,

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:12.527
we are, at least in my friend group,

00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:18.575
I'm finding that people are yearning
for more and more intimacy, vulnerability,

00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:20.577
you know, dinner parties,

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:23.788
like, I'm feeling a pendulum shift back

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.792
because I feel like people have been
so digitally focused for so long.

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:31.421
And no, I don't feel that way at all.

00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:34.758
JJ: I get that, and I think
the pendulum is definitely shifting.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.927
I guess when I read this,
the editor in me is like,

00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:39.596
well, what do you mean by real?

00:01:39.638 --> 00:01:43.058
So I think for some folks,

00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:47.687
just being able to text you
when they’re freaking out

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:52.025
and just have that little human
conversation is real connectivity

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:56.613
because it sort of paves the way
for a more open conversation.

00:01:56.655 --> 00:02:00.367
Some people aren’t ready
to have the eye-to-eye,

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.662
face-to-face like, conversation,

00:02:03.703 --> 00:02:08.416
but they could chat you up
all day on WhatsApp

00:02:08.458 --> 00:02:10.669
and that's about as real as they can get.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.422
And I don't know, but I suspect
some people kind of need

00:02:14.422 --> 00:02:19.594
to be walked from digital
and virtual into real world,

00:02:19.636 --> 00:02:21.596
into real space, real time.

00:02:21.638 --> 00:02:24.557
And maybe that's what's
a "real" conversation for them.

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:26.726
It kind of pulls them
towards something else.

00:02:26.726 --> 00:02:28.728
BM: Yeah, and I also, I was just thinking,

00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:31.606
I’m probably
more exposed to that

00:02:31.606 --> 00:02:34.275
because most of the work I do
is in mental health now.

00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:37.779
And so most of the people I’m talking to,
it’s about their mental health

00:02:37.821 --> 00:02:40.532
and their opening up
about their mental health journeys.

00:02:40.573 --> 00:02:46.996
And so I'm kind of predisposed to a lot
of very vulnerable, very honest,

00:02:47.038 --> 00:02:48.331
very raw conversations,

00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:50.792
probably more than the general population.

00:02:50.792 --> 00:02:53.878
JJ: I imagine that some people start
to have those conversations,

00:02:53.878 --> 00:02:56.756
their capacity for it grows
and maybe their desire for it.

00:02:56.798 --> 00:03:00.427
Like, once you get the benefit of like,
oh, that's what that was, then --

00:03:00.427 --> 00:03:01.594
BM: You want more of it.

00:03:01.636 --> 00:03:04.431
JJ: You want more of it,
and you find the facility with it.

00:03:04.431 --> 00:03:05.890
For liability purposes,

00:03:05.932 --> 00:03:11.146
I'm going to start
with a gentle swish and flick.

00:03:11.896 --> 00:03:13.857
BM: OK, this is good.

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:19.195
So many men feel lonelier than ever
and don't know how to talk to one another.

00:03:19.237 --> 00:03:20.613
Where do they start?

00:03:23.074 --> 00:03:28.872
JJ: I think that they might
have to start inward,

00:03:28.872 --> 00:03:31.291
because if you're not
comfortable saying, "Hey man,

00:03:31.332 --> 00:03:35.420
I'm not sure how to
connect to other people,"

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.174
that's a level of exposure
I'm not sure some guys are ready for,

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:41.843
if you're already struggling
to connect to other people.

00:03:41.843 --> 00:03:43.303
I think for some men,

00:03:43.344 --> 00:03:46.347
maybe that first conversation
needs to be in the mirror

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:48.641
And be like, "Look,
this is what my life is,

00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:50.101
there's no use kidding myself.

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:51.478
Here's where I really am.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:54.439
This is what's happening,
this is what my social life is like,

00:03:54.481 --> 00:03:56.316
this is what my romantic life is like,

00:03:56.316 --> 00:03:57.525
this is how I see myself,

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.068
this is what I actually want."

00:03:59.068 --> 00:04:02.906
And figure out if I had more connection,

00:04:02.947 --> 00:04:05.158
what would that mean to me?

00:04:05.158 --> 00:04:08.119
What would a perfect day
in my life look like

00:04:08.119 --> 00:04:09.829
if I had the connections I wanted?

00:04:10.288 --> 00:04:12.707
Rather than just, “Well
you should reach out more.

00:04:12.707 --> 00:04:14.167
You should be more connected."

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:15.877
Why? To what end?

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:18.546
I think a lot of guys, that I know anyway,

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:21.508
rebel against that
because they're not sure

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.676
like, when you're done,

00:04:23.718 --> 00:04:26.596
like, when do I know if this works?

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:31.518
Just kind of sitting in the discomfort
and like, "OK, am I social now?"

00:04:31.559 --> 00:04:32.769
Like, "Am I human now?"

00:04:32.769 --> 00:04:34.687
It just feels too fuzzy.

00:04:35.021 --> 00:04:38.149
So maybe the first thing they need
to do is get it clear in here

00:04:38.191 --> 00:04:40.860
so that they have a sense of agency.

00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:42.737
When they're asked to go do something,

00:04:42.737 --> 00:04:45.240
they can go, "That's not
really what I'm looking for"

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.450
and mean it as opposed to, “Oh, do that.”

00:04:47.492 --> 00:04:50.787
Like, “My goal is to make
this change in my life

00:04:50.829 --> 00:04:52.413
that would look this way

00:04:52.413 --> 00:04:54.916
and that doesn't seem
like it meets this goal

00:04:54.958 --> 00:04:56.626
so I'm going to pass."

00:04:56.668 --> 00:04:59.045
A little more actively than just being

00:04:59.045 --> 00:05:01.464
kind of like, blown sideways through.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.259
"You should be more social
for socializing sake,"

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.468
you know what I mean?

00:05:05.468 --> 00:05:09.430
BM: Yeah, I mean, I was thinking of it
just like a very practical tip.

00:05:09.472 --> 00:05:11.266
If someone is lonely

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:13.726
and they don't know
how to talk to another man,

00:05:13.726 --> 00:05:17.522
I think the easiest thing
is to invite someone to coffee

00:05:17.564 --> 00:05:19.274
and ask them about their life.

00:05:19.691 --> 00:05:23.444
Because I find that if someone
is hesitant or scared

00:05:23.444 --> 00:05:27.323
or doesn't know how
to be vulnerable with another man,

00:05:27.323 --> 00:05:31.035
it's much easier
if someone has gone first.

00:05:31.411 --> 00:05:34.706
But if you really are feeling
the need to do that,

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:37.250
then by inviting someone
to coffee, you know,

00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:40.295
ideally someone you
have been close to before,

00:05:40.336 --> 00:05:42.630
and just saying, "Hey,
I just want to check in,"

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.383
like, "How is everything
going in your life?"

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:48.136
That guy is most likely going to share

00:05:48.177 --> 00:05:52.140
and then that will make it easier
for then you to share.

00:05:52.181 --> 00:05:55.101
So I guess I’m going to
[answer] this question

00:05:55.143 --> 00:05:58.646
much more practical [than]
someone watching this right now.

00:05:58.688 --> 00:06:02.233
They’re like, “Man, I feel like
I don’t have any good guy friends anymore.

00:06:02.275 --> 00:06:04.819
I don't feel like I'm connected
to my buddies at all."

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:07.322
And actually, I have
a short little story about this,

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:08.531
which is interesting.

00:06:08.531 --> 00:06:11.075
I was on my way home
for Thanksgiving and I went home,

00:06:11.075 --> 00:06:13.620
and I was at the time --
gosh, it was 19 years ago.

00:06:13.661 --> 00:06:15.830
So I was like 29, 30 years old.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:19.918
And I said, "Dad, after Thanksgiving,
can we go into the study

00:06:19.959 --> 00:06:22.086
and I want to interview you
about your life."

00:06:22.128 --> 00:06:24.213
My dad was like, "OK," we were close.

00:06:24.213 --> 00:06:26.925
And so I asked my dad
all these questions about his life,

00:06:26.966 --> 00:06:28.676
and it was just a great experience.

00:06:28.676 --> 00:06:30.970
But at the end, I asked him
that final question.

00:06:31.012 --> 00:06:34.223
I said, "Dad, [do] you have any regrets?"

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:38.394
And he said, "Blake,
I do have one regret."

00:06:38.436 --> 00:06:40.021
I said, "OK, what is it?"

00:06:40.021 --> 00:06:41.522
He said, "Look, your mom and I

00:06:41.522 --> 00:06:44.525
have had this beautiful
marriage for now, 37 years.

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:47.195
You, your sister and your brother
have turned out great.

00:06:47.236 --> 00:06:50.114
You're all doing different things
and following your passion.

00:06:50.156 --> 00:06:53.993
I've loved my medical practice
and helping people.

00:06:54.035 --> 00:06:57.789
But over the years, I really
lost touch with all my guy friends.

00:06:58.206 --> 00:07:02.085
You know, all my friends from college,
all my friends from medical school.

00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:03.878
You know, I always just said no

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.507
to the weekend golf guys trips
or to this or that,

00:07:07.507 --> 00:07:10.969
because I was just so busy
with you and your mom and my work.

00:07:10.969 --> 00:07:14.138
And now you guys are in college
or out of the house.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.808
You know, I'm now
getting close to retirement.

00:07:16.808 --> 00:07:19.727
And I love your mom,
but I can't spend every moment with her.

00:07:19.727 --> 00:07:22.146
And so I don't really have
anyone to hang out with.

00:07:22.146 --> 00:07:23.439
And I'm lonely."

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.484
And he said, "So don't make that mistake."

00:07:26.526 --> 00:07:28.903
And so at that time, I was 30 years old.

00:07:28.903 --> 00:07:31.739
I had just started Toms Shoes --
we were like two years old.

00:07:31.739 --> 00:07:35.410
And I wrote an email
to my seven best friends --

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.038
a couple of them from college,
some were newer friends in LA --

00:07:39.038 --> 00:07:40.873
and I told them the story, and I said,

00:07:40.873 --> 00:07:42.500
"We can't have this happen to us.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.544
Let's do an annual trip every year."

00:07:44.585 --> 00:07:47.630
And so we started
our first trip 19 years ago,

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:50.133
and we're getting ready
to have our 20th anniversary.

00:07:50.174 --> 00:07:51.592
JJ: Where was the first trip?

00:07:51.592 --> 00:07:54.512
BM: The first trip was a camping
and fishing trip in Colorado.

00:07:54.554 --> 00:07:58.141
And we've done one trip
every single year for 19 years.

00:07:58.182 --> 00:08:00.268
The group is now 14 guys.

00:08:00.309 --> 00:08:02.353
We call it the “Rugged Adventure Club.”

00:08:02.395 --> 00:08:03.604
It was rugged,

00:08:03.646 --> 00:08:06.107
and then when I sold Toms,
it became not so rugged.

00:08:06.107 --> 00:08:08.651
We started going to the Maldives
and all these places.

00:08:08.693 --> 00:08:11.821
JJ: You can't call it like,
"the guys who glamp," it doesn't ring.

00:08:11.821 --> 00:08:12.989
BM: Exactly.

00:08:12.989 --> 00:08:16.743
But I bring that up because, you know,

00:08:16.784 --> 00:08:21.914
that group has taught me
how to have really meaningful,

00:08:21.956 --> 00:08:23.791
deep relationships with men,

00:08:23.791 --> 00:08:26.586
which has had probably as big of an impact

00:08:26.627 --> 00:08:28.629
on my mental health as anything else.

00:08:29.255 --> 00:08:34.052
JJ: I love that story because your dad
had the presence of mind

00:08:34.052 --> 00:08:39.390
and the willingness
to be vulnerable with his regrets.

00:08:40.433 --> 00:08:42.393
Can I tell you my version of that story?

00:08:42.393 --> 00:08:43.770
BM: Sure, I'm here for it.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.064
JJ: My father, you know,

00:08:46.105 --> 00:08:49.442
my father passed away last year
and was ill for a very long time.

00:08:49.484 --> 00:08:52.320
His health declined, declined,
declined, declined for years.

00:08:52.361 --> 00:08:54.947
And he and I were really quite estranged.

00:08:54.989 --> 00:08:58.367
You know, he and my mother
divorced when I was five,

00:08:58.409 --> 00:09:00.661
the estrangement deepened
after I came out.

00:09:00.703 --> 00:09:02.705
And so it was just kind of like a process.

00:09:02.747 --> 00:09:04.582
And my father was a Jehovah's Witness,

00:09:04.582 --> 00:09:06.459
so it was just one thing after another.

00:09:06.501 --> 00:09:09.587
Eventually I kind of felt
convicted and I was like,

00:09:09.629 --> 00:09:11.756
I need to keep trying to reach out.

00:09:11.798 --> 00:09:14.050
And I tried to reach out
and tried to reach out.

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.219
Finally, kind of
in the last year of his life,

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.678
he was really not doing well.

00:09:17.678 --> 00:09:22.725
And I thought, I need to make time
to fly home to Florida

00:09:22.725 --> 00:09:25.144
and just sit down and talk to the guy.

00:09:25.812 --> 00:09:29.357
And I said, "Dad,
I want to do your oral history.

00:09:29.398 --> 00:09:30.942
I feel like I don't know you.

00:09:30.983 --> 00:09:32.527
Like, not really know you."

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:35.279
Made a special trip,
planned the whole thing.

00:09:35.321 --> 00:09:37.115
"Dad, are we still going to do this?"

00:09:37.115 --> 00:09:38.741
"Yup, I'll see you in three days."

00:09:38.783 --> 00:09:42.411
I flew down, checked in the hotel,
got up the next day, went to visit my mom,

00:09:42.411 --> 00:09:43.579
"Dad, are we still on?"

00:09:43.621 --> 00:09:45.331
"Yep, I'll see you."

00:09:45.373 --> 00:09:47.083
Another day: "Dad?" "Yep."

00:09:47.125 --> 00:09:48.626
"Tomorrow. Do it."

00:09:48.668 --> 00:09:50.962
The morning comes and I call him.

00:09:51.003 --> 00:09:52.755
"OK, dad. I'm ready to go.

00:09:52.755 --> 00:09:54.549
When should I come meet you?"

00:09:55.466 --> 00:09:57.051
"You know ...

00:09:59.011 --> 00:10:00.513
We've got time.

00:10:00.555 --> 00:10:02.390
I don't want you to put yourself out

00:10:02.431 --> 00:10:05.143
and, like, I'm not really
feeling up to it.

00:10:05.184 --> 00:10:08.187
Well don’t worry about it,
but we’ve got time.”

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.149
It's like, "Dad, I booked
a flight for you.

00:10:11.607 --> 00:10:12.775
I bought equipment.

00:10:12.775 --> 00:10:14.777
I'm ready to do this now."

00:10:14.819 --> 00:10:15.987
"You know what?

00:10:16.028 --> 00:10:18.364
Next time you come down, let me know."

00:10:18.364 --> 00:10:19.574
BM: Oh, no.

00:10:19.574 --> 00:10:21.450
JJ: "We'll do it then, we've got time."

00:10:21.492 --> 00:10:22.743
BM: Oh, no.

00:10:22.785 --> 00:10:25.246
JJ: And in that moment,
I decided, I'm done.

00:10:25.913 --> 00:10:27.540
I'm not going to ask anymore.

00:10:28.332 --> 00:10:31.169
I don't know what it was,

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:35.840
but I think my father was unable

00:10:35.882 --> 00:10:38.509
to really process being a dad

00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:40.261
beyond being a provider.

00:10:41.137 --> 00:10:45.808
And that human connection of like,
"this is how to be a man,

00:10:45.808 --> 00:10:47.727
this is how to be a Black man,"

00:10:47.727 --> 00:10:50.897
never quite clicked for him,
even after I became a storyteller.

00:10:50.938 --> 00:10:53.399
He knew I was a journalist,
very proud of my work,

00:10:53.441 --> 00:10:54.859
but couldn’t quite get

00:10:54.901 --> 00:10:57.987
[that] I don’t need
what you can provide for me.

00:10:58.029 --> 00:10:59.530
I need you.

00:11:00.406 --> 00:11:05.536
And when he passed,
I was supposed to like, write his obit.

00:11:06.204 --> 00:11:07.580
Still haven't.

00:11:07.622 --> 00:11:09.457
Because I don't know what to write.

00:11:11.876 --> 00:11:13.628
BM: OK, so this question is great.

00:11:14.837 --> 00:11:17.882
I'm assuming it's the one
we're already having, but you tell me,

00:11:17.882 --> 00:11:20.843
what's the most transformational
conversation you've ever had?

00:11:21.219 --> 00:11:23.304
JJ: Oh, God, I had so many.

00:11:23.346 --> 00:11:25.348
I mean, I was an interviewer for a living,

00:11:25.348 --> 00:11:27.225
so I've had way too many.

00:11:27.266 --> 00:11:30.728
I think the biggest collection
of transformational conversations

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:33.439
I ever had was when I was
a national host on NPR.

00:11:33.814 --> 00:11:35.900
I got to interview Eric Holder,

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:39.320
the former US Attorney
General under Barack Obama.

00:11:39.904 --> 00:11:43.699
And I was really excited to interview him
because it was one of the first people

00:11:43.699 --> 00:11:45.743
of his echelon that I had ever talked to.

00:11:45.743 --> 00:11:46.953
I was really excited.

00:11:46.994 --> 00:11:48.371
And then after a while,

00:11:48.371 --> 00:11:51.165
I could kind of hear
my goofy family in the background

00:11:51.207 --> 00:11:55.169
because we're not highfalutin,
we're not fancy people,

00:11:55.211 --> 00:11:56.379
very together,

00:11:56.379 --> 00:11:58.631
but we don’t put on
airs and attitudes

00:11:58.673 --> 00:12:00.299
and something about it felt weird.

00:12:00.299 --> 00:12:03.719
Especially because I wasn't thinking
anything about the guest after him.

00:12:03.719 --> 00:12:04.887
It was nobody famous.

00:12:04.929 --> 00:12:06.639
And so I decided to like, OK,

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:10.226
I'm just going to talk to him
like he's any other guest.

00:12:10.559 --> 00:12:11.769
Invite him in,

00:12:11.811 --> 00:12:13.271
"How's it going? Have a seat."

00:12:13.271 --> 00:12:15.106
Make comfortable, very personable guy.

00:12:15.147 --> 00:12:16.315
Easy to talk to.

00:12:16.315 --> 00:12:17.900
Segment starts.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.402
Break comes.

00:12:19.443 --> 00:12:21.821
His assistant comes in to remind him,

00:12:21.821 --> 00:12:24.699
"Sir, we're going to leave halfway
through the next segment."

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:28.494
We're going to play music and he was going
to be able to slip out of the studio.

00:12:28.494 --> 00:12:32.623
He looks over his shoulder
at his assistant and is like,

00:12:32.623 --> 00:12:34.792
"I think we can stay a few more minutes."

00:12:35.334 --> 00:12:37.837
Extended the interview on the spot

00:12:37.878 --> 00:12:39.505
because he was having a good time.

00:12:39.505 --> 00:12:40.673
BM: Yeah, yeah.

00:12:40.715 --> 00:12:43.551
JJ: And I was, you know,
smelling myself for a second, like,

00:12:43.551 --> 00:12:45.636
"That's right, I'd extended it
with me too."

00:12:45.636 --> 00:12:49.223
But then after it was over, I thought
like, dang, I made the right choice.

00:12:50.141 --> 00:12:51.309
And from that,

00:12:51.309 --> 00:12:56.314
I kind of reframed the way
that I deal with just about everybody.

00:12:56.314 --> 00:13:03.237
I mean, if you were going to treat
every person you will ever meet

00:13:03.237 --> 00:13:07.575
for the rest of your life
exactly the same,

00:13:07.616 --> 00:13:08.951
how would you treat them?

00:13:09.744 --> 00:13:11.245
That's what that taught me.

00:13:11.287 --> 00:13:15.249
Is to be one person,
to set one standard, raise or lower,

00:13:15.249 --> 00:13:17.001
some people you're going to embrace,

00:13:17.001 --> 00:13:19.420
and some people are going
to drive you up the wall.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.507
But it sort of made every interview
that followed easier

00:13:23.507 --> 00:13:26.093
because I didn't have to calibrate myself.

00:13:27.136 --> 00:13:31.265
It’s like conversation no longer depended
on anything around me.

00:13:31.307 --> 00:13:33.267
It depended on everything within me.

00:13:33.309 --> 00:13:34.894
Like I know who I am as a person,

00:13:34.894 --> 00:13:36.604
I know what my values are,

00:13:36.645 --> 00:13:39.023
I know how I want to be treated.

00:13:39.065 --> 00:13:40.816
That rule is golden for a reason.

00:13:41.233 --> 00:13:43.235
And I decided that's who I'm going to be.

00:13:43.235 --> 00:13:44.945
And if we don't vibe, that's cool.

00:13:44.945 --> 00:13:46.572
If we do, that's fine.

00:13:46.572 --> 00:13:52.203
But it just made life so much easier
and it made the work easier.

00:13:52.244 --> 00:13:54.330
Like, this is me.

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:56.499
Oh, we don't click? OK, cool, that's cool.

00:13:56.499 --> 00:13:58.167
You get to be you, I get to be me.

00:13:58.167 --> 00:14:00.294
And we figure it out
somewhere along the way.

00:14:00.336 --> 00:14:02.171
And through all of the ups and downs

00:14:02.213 --> 00:14:04.298
of talking to all kinds
of different people,

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:05.800
that was just like,

00:14:05.841 --> 00:14:09.887
it just greased the wheels
for the next three years of that work.

00:14:09.929 --> 00:14:11.097
It was ...

00:14:11.806 --> 00:14:13.516
I'm amazed I thought of it.

00:14:13.557 --> 00:14:15.184
I didn't know I was that bright.

00:14:15.226 --> 00:14:19.438
I'm glad I had that burst of insight
because it made life so much simpler,

00:14:19.438 --> 00:14:20.856
so much simpler.

00:14:20.898 --> 00:14:22.566
BM: I love that, I love that.

00:14:23.234 --> 00:14:24.527
OK, so for me,

00:14:24.568 --> 00:14:27.488
what's the most transformational
conversation you've ever had?

00:14:27.488 --> 00:14:31.700
It actually happened
about a year ago, with my kids.

00:14:32.201 --> 00:14:35.287
And I have an 11-year-old
and an eight-year-old.

00:14:35.287 --> 00:14:38.416
And also a 31-year-old adopted son.

00:14:38.457 --> 00:14:40.668
But this was with the biological kids.

00:14:40.668 --> 00:14:43.963
And they had seen, you know,

00:14:44.004 --> 00:14:47.299
and experienced me having a really
hard mental health crisis

00:14:47.341 --> 00:14:48.509
for about two years.

00:14:48.509 --> 00:14:50.344
I was in and out of treatment centers,

00:14:50.344 --> 00:14:52.930
I wasn't able to
kind of, be there for them

00:14:52.972 --> 00:14:55.599
in the way that I was before,
it was a really hard time.

00:14:55.641 --> 00:14:57.852
And then I started feeling better.

00:14:57.852 --> 00:15:01.188
I had some amazing healing experiences,

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.983
especially around this core wound
of not feeling enough.

00:15:04.442 --> 00:15:08.946
And as I started feeling better,
they could tell it was really different.

00:15:08.988 --> 00:15:11.198
And now I'm spending
a lot more time with them.

00:15:11.198 --> 00:15:14.410
And the conversation
I had with them is one day,

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:18.247
my son said something
about like, "What's different?"

00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:21.625
And kind of like, well,

00:15:21.667 --> 00:15:24.628
"How do we know you're not
going to feel bad again" type thing?

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:25.838
He was worried.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:28.424
I said, “Because now
I know that I’m enough,

00:15:28.424 --> 00:15:29.842
just as I am."

00:15:30.342 --> 00:15:33.137
Not because of what I've done,
not because of what I built,

00:15:33.137 --> 00:15:35.181
not because of what everyone thinks of me,

00:15:35.222 --> 00:15:36.432
but just because I exist.

00:15:36.474 --> 00:15:38.309
And now that I know that,

00:15:38.309 --> 00:15:40.519
I think my life will always be different.

00:15:40.561 --> 00:15:42.855
And I saw him like, look.

00:15:42.897 --> 00:15:44.523
And it was the coolest thing

00:15:44.565 --> 00:15:47.943
because I then continued
the conversation like,

00:15:47.943 --> 00:15:50.863
"And that extends to you
and your sister too.

00:15:50.863 --> 00:15:53.240
You guys are enough exactly as you are.

00:15:53.282 --> 00:15:55.910
You can try out for the water polo team,

00:15:55.951 --> 00:15:57.620
you can try to make good grades,

00:15:57.661 --> 00:15:59.997
You can try to do these things.

00:16:00.039 --> 00:16:04.043
But none of that is tied to your worth.

00:16:04.502 --> 00:16:07.963
I love you, and your mom loves you
and the world loves you.

00:16:07.963 --> 00:16:10.925
And they always will
just because you exist."

00:16:11.592 --> 00:16:14.845
And ever since then,
I've seen a lightness in my kids

00:16:14.845 --> 00:16:16.764
and a connection in us

00:16:16.805 --> 00:16:20.267
that is so deep, so peaceful,

00:16:20.267 --> 00:16:24.146
so much that at dinner every night
we have this little ritual

00:16:24.146 --> 00:16:27.274
where we hold hands and my eight-year-old,

00:16:27.316 --> 00:16:30.236
we start with the youngest first,
and she says, "I am enough,"

00:16:30.277 --> 00:16:32.321
and looks around and then she looks at us,

00:16:32.321 --> 00:16:33.614
she goes, "You are enough,"

00:16:33.656 --> 00:16:35.282
and she goes, "And we are enough."

00:16:35.324 --> 00:16:37.660
And then the next child does it,
and then I do it.

00:16:37.701 --> 00:16:39.662
And that all came from that conversation.

00:16:39.703 --> 00:16:41.956
And I hadn't even thought
about that until this.

00:16:41.997 --> 00:16:45.334
But it was one of probably the most
important conversations of my life.

00:16:45.376 --> 00:16:47.294
JJ: Two questions I have to ask on that.

00:16:47.294 --> 00:16:49.672
When you said you saw
a lightness in your kid,

00:16:49.713 --> 00:16:53.759
was there anything in particular
that you were concerned about

00:16:53.801 --> 00:16:55.094
getting heavy for them?

00:16:55.135 --> 00:16:57.304
Like, were there certain
aspects of growing up

00:16:57.304 --> 00:16:59.473
that you thought they needed
that message for?

00:16:59.515 --> 00:17:02.059
BM: I think when someone
does not feel enough,

00:17:02.059 --> 00:17:04.520
which I didn't for 48 years of my life,

00:17:04.562 --> 00:17:09.191
then I think you subconsciously
project that onto others

00:17:09.191 --> 00:17:11.110
and especially parents to kids.

00:17:11.151 --> 00:17:12.820
So if a parent doesn't feel enough

00:17:12.861 --> 00:17:16.407
and doesn't have their own sense of worth
outside of their accomplishments,

00:17:16.448 --> 00:17:19.451
then you're projecting
that pressure onto the kids.

00:17:19.493 --> 00:17:23.163
So I think my son was feeling like he had
to become a great water polo player,

00:17:23.205 --> 00:17:25.332
or had to be the star
of the basketball team,

00:17:25.332 --> 00:17:26.750
or had to make great grades,

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:29.670
or had to look a certain way
because I felt that about myself.

00:17:29.712 --> 00:17:32.047
I never said that to him.

00:17:32.047 --> 00:17:33.465
JJ: You don't have to say it.

00:17:33.465 --> 00:17:34.842
BM: I didn't have to say it.

00:17:34.842 --> 00:17:37.845
But as soon as I felt that I was enough
and I said that to them,

00:17:37.886 --> 00:17:41.724
then I saw him reduce the pressure
he was putting on himself.

00:17:41.765 --> 00:17:46.020
And my daughter still, I think,
has somewhere to grow in that,

00:17:46.061 --> 00:17:49.189
because I still think she puts
a lot of pressure on herself still.

00:17:49.189 --> 00:17:50.482
I mean, she's only eight.

00:17:50.524 --> 00:17:54.486
But I have seen that when
we do the ritual at dinner,

00:17:54.486 --> 00:17:56.989
there's like a twinkle
in their eye, there's a smile,

00:17:57.031 --> 00:17:58.782
there's like, "Yeah, I am enough."

00:17:58.782 --> 00:17:59.992
And it's a practice.

00:17:59.992 --> 00:18:01.201
It's not something like,

00:18:01.201 --> 00:18:03.704
oh, I experienced it once
and now I have it forever.

00:18:03.746 --> 00:18:06.707
It's something that you have to continue
to practice every day.

00:18:06.749 --> 00:18:08.709
You know, that's why I wear the bracelet.

00:18:08.709 --> 00:18:12.046
It's my reminder every day
to go back to this centering.

00:18:12.087 --> 00:18:14.214
But that conversation with the kids,

00:18:14.256 --> 00:18:18.177
I think will be one that I'm willing
to bet any amount of money

00:18:18.177 --> 00:18:20.346
if they're sitting
in these chairs someday,

00:18:20.387 --> 00:18:22.139
at least my son would say

00:18:22.139 --> 00:18:24.558
that was probably the most
meaningful conversation.

00:18:24.558 --> 00:18:26.560
JJ: I see you have two of those bracelets,

00:18:26.602 --> 00:18:28.479
and I feel like when you said enough --

00:18:28.479 --> 00:18:29.855
BM: You know why I have two?

00:18:29.855 --> 00:18:31.065
JJ: Why?

00:18:31.106 --> 00:18:33.525
BM: You'll see. So one
of my favorite things to do,

00:18:33.567 --> 00:18:35.277
it's not about wearing it yourself.

00:18:35.277 --> 00:18:37.071
It's about giving it to someone else.

00:18:37.071 --> 00:18:38.530
So I always wear an extra one.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.532
So if I ever feel like, OK,

00:18:40.574 --> 00:18:42.785
this is something I want
to share with someone,

00:18:42.785 --> 00:18:44.536
which I do right now, I can give it.

00:18:44.536 --> 00:18:46.622
So hold out your hand, palm up.

00:18:46.664 --> 00:18:47.790
There you go.

00:18:47.831 --> 00:18:49.958
And it says "you are enough"
on the inside.

00:18:49.958 --> 00:18:51.168
JJ: Oh, wow.

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.461
BM: And then ...

00:18:53.796 --> 00:18:55.506
Now you have one, too.

00:18:55.506 --> 00:18:56.924
JJ: I love it, thank you.

00:18:56.965 --> 00:18:58.133
BM: You're welcome.

00:18:58.133 --> 00:18:59.343
JJ: Thank you so much.

00:18:59.343 --> 00:19:01.095
When I saw this, I kind of saw this

00:19:01.095 --> 00:19:03.389
with a period and also
with an exclamation point.

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:04.556
BM: It can be both.

00:19:04.598 --> 00:19:05.766
JJ: Like, enough!

00:19:05.808 --> 00:19:08.811
As sort of a check point.

00:19:08.811 --> 00:19:10.020
BM: Boundary.

00:19:10.062 --> 00:19:13.691
JJ: Or enough, like a relief almost.

00:19:13.732 --> 00:19:17.736
BM: It's interesting you bring that up
because I originally made it,

00:19:17.736 --> 00:19:20.447
we hand-make them,
these women in India make them.

00:19:21.281 --> 00:19:23.742
And that's where I had
a bunch of my healing.

00:19:23.784 --> 00:19:29.039
And originally, it was more
the "I am enough," a softness.

00:19:29.039 --> 00:19:33.127
But I also think in the climate
that we live in today,

00:19:33.168 --> 00:19:35.963
there is something also
that's a real activist spirit.

00:19:36.338 --> 00:19:37.631
So it's like, enough!

00:19:37.631 --> 00:19:40.634
And almost even like, I think of,

00:19:40.676 --> 00:19:43.679
you know, people who sometimes
have been told [in] their life

00:19:43.721 --> 00:19:46.181
that they're not smart enough
or pretty enough

00:19:46.181 --> 00:19:48.350
or that they're going to amount to enough.

00:19:48.392 --> 00:19:51.603
It's almost like
a "fuck you" to all the people

00:19:51.645 --> 00:19:53.355
who said you couldn't do it,

00:19:53.355 --> 00:19:56.442
that you are enough
and you don't need their validation.

00:19:56.442 --> 00:19:57.609
JJ: I love it.

00:19:57.609 --> 00:19:58.861
Thank you for this.

00:19:58.902 --> 00:20:00.070
BM: OK, let's see.

00:20:00.446 --> 00:20:01.739
Where did that go?

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:02.990
JJ: Right.

00:20:04.324 --> 00:20:05.492
It's the daily double.

00:20:05.534 --> 00:20:06.702
No.

00:20:08.579 --> 00:20:12.332
If you could redesign
how people connect with each other,

00:20:12.332 --> 00:20:13.876
where would you start?

00:20:17.171 --> 00:20:18.422
BM: If I could --

00:20:18.464 --> 00:20:19.882
JJ: Wait. You went somewhere.

00:20:19.882 --> 00:20:21.049
Where did you go?

00:20:21.049 --> 00:20:22.676
BM: This is exactly where I went.

00:20:22.968 --> 00:20:24.845
I went to "Avatar."

00:20:25.512 --> 00:20:27.431
JJ: I knew there was something specific.

00:20:27.473 --> 00:20:28.932
BM: And Pandora.

00:20:28.974 --> 00:20:30.768
And if you've watched "Avatar,"

00:20:30.809 --> 00:20:36.190
when the Avatar gets on the horse,
then they plug in the hair --

00:20:36.231 --> 00:20:37.441
JJ: Yeah.

00:20:37.483 --> 00:20:38.776
BM: So that's where I went.

00:20:38.817 --> 00:20:40.277
JJ: Where they kind of like --

00:20:40.277 --> 00:20:43.489
BM: Yeah. And then I was like,
if I could design any way to connect,

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:47.034
there'd be some way
that like, when we shook hands,

00:20:47.034 --> 00:20:50.621
I transferred part
of my consciousness to you.

00:20:50.621 --> 00:20:53.165
And so we connected in a nonverbal way.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:56.877
And we could have
conversation through touch

00:20:56.919 --> 00:20:58.712
without having to use our words.

00:20:59.254 --> 00:21:00.923
That's where I just went.

00:21:00.964 --> 00:21:02.800
JJ: But, you know, I get it.

00:21:02.841 --> 00:21:04.468
I knew you saw something specific.

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:06.845
I could see your eyes
moving behind your eyebrows.

00:21:06.845 --> 00:21:08.722
If I could redesign how people connect,

00:21:08.764 --> 00:21:11.850
I would make it
an educational prerequisite.

00:21:11.892 --> 00:21:15.187
I would put it into schools
because that's within our power.

00:21:15.187 --> 00:21:19.608
I would put listening, communication,

00:21:19.608 --> 00:21:23.111
and critical thinking as a high school
graduation requirement.

00:21:23.111 --> 00:21:24.404
BM: Yeah, beautiful.

00:21:25.197 --> 00:21:27.324
JJ: I would make it
a Common Core standard.

00:21:27.366 --> 00:21:28.992
I would put it in the Common Core,

00:21:29.034 --> 00:21:32.329
especially because when you’re little,

00:21:32.371 --> 00:21:34.832
like, kids [have] great questions.

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:39.711
Kids are built-in question-askers,

00:21:39.753 --> 00:21:43.006
idea finder-outers
and they’re heuristic learning machines

00:21:43.006 --> 00:21:45.968
and they'll pepper you
with a million questions.

00:21:45.968 --> 00:21:49.179
But that's exactly where,
as a living thing,

00:21:49.221 --> 00:21:50.639
your first job is to connect.

00:21:50.681 --> 00:21:53.267
The first thing a baby does
is connect with its mother.

00:21:53.267 --> 00:21:55.060
And so, connection is our lifeblood.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:58.522
And I think if you start real young,

00:21:58.564 --> 00:22:01.608
I mean, I did speech and debate
in middle and high school.

00:22:01.608 --> 00:22:03.902
[The] best skill set I ever learned --

00:22:03.902 --> 00:22:07.322
as much as I use math and science,
writing, social studies --

00:22:07.364 --> 00:22:10.909
the best skill set I ever learned
was how to communicate with other people,

00:22:10.909 --> 00:22:12.870
because that's how you connect.

00:22:12.911 --> 00:22:15.956
Every piece of the social crisis
we see with young people --

00:22:15.956 --> 00:22:18.458
computers and AI and TikTok --

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.460
it all comes back to human connection.

00:22:20.502 --> 00:22:25.382
They're trying to connect,
but you can't do it 14 seconds at a time.

00:22:25.799 --> 00:22:27.509
You've got to sort of invest in it.

00:22:27.509 --> 00:22:30.679
And I think kids are sort of
crying out for it.

00:22:30.721 --> 00:22:32.514
They know something's wrong.

00:22:32.514 --> 00:22:35.809
They know that their parents
and grandparents did not go through this,

00:22:35.851 --> 00:22:38.478
but they don't know why they have to.

00:22:39.104 --> 00:22:40.814
They’re aware of what’s happening,

00:22:40.856 --> 00:22:44.651
and frankly, are already
starting to get a little scared about it.

00:22:44.693 --> 00:22:48.864
I read a study last week that said
Gen Z is saving for retirement

00:22:48.864 --> 00:22:52.200
at higher rates than baby boomers
did at their age.

00:22:52.242 --> 00:22:55.037
So they're aware the world
is not built for them right now,

00:22:55.037 --> 00:22:58.040
and they're going to want
to find solace in one another.

00:22:58.707 --> 00:23:01.710
I think if we teach kids to do it,
they'll stick to it.

00:23:01.752 --> 00:23:05.797
They'll learn what
appropriate conversations are,

00:23:05.839 --> 00:23:09.134
listening, investing in people,
even eye contact.

00:23:09.635 --> 00:23:12.179
Even just something as simple
as you and me being able

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:14.431
to look at each other
and have the conversation

00:23:14.431 --> 00:23:17.517
and not feel like, you know,
we're either staring each other down

00:23:17.559 --> 00:23:20.395
or like, waiting for one
of our heads to explode.

00:23:20.896 --> 00:23:24.316
That skill set for kids is just ...

00:23:25.943 --> 00:23:28.654
It revolutionizes
what it's like to grow up

00:23:28.654 --> 00:23:30.447
and to become a citizen of the world.

00:23:30.447 --> 00:23:33.450
I would start by making it
a Common Core standard

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:35.869
and making it a high school
requirement in schools.

00:23:35.869 --> 00:23:37.245
BM: That's great, I love it.

00:23:37.788 --> 00:23:40.791
JJ: Now I'm going to throw it
where I ain't supposed to throw it

00:23:40.791 --> 00:23:43.126
and see if it ends up
where it's supposed to be.

00:23:45.212 --> 00:23:46.672
BM: Oh!

00:23:46.672 --> 00:23:48.382
JJ: See that?

00:23:48.715 --> 00:23:49.883
BM: OK, that was good.

00:23:49.925 --> 00:23:52.427
JJ: They set you up for failure
and you find success.

00:23:52.427 --> 00:23:54.429
BM: OK, we only have two left.

00:23:54.846 --> 00:23:56.056
Here we go.

00:23:56.056 --> 00:23:58.392
JJ: But first, let's see
the one you didn't pick.

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.812
I'll ask you and you can put
the answer in the comments.

00:24:01.853 --> 00:24:03.021
How about that?

00:24:03.021 --> 00:24:04.231
The one we didn't pick.

00:24:04.231 --> 00:24:08.026
When did you realize
that strength and vulnerability

00:24:08.026 --> 00:24:10.112
were not opposites?

00:24:10.988 --> 00:24:12.114
Put it in the comments.

00:24:12.155 --> 00:24:14.241
BM: So the last question today is,

00:24:14.282 --> 00:24:18.912
what are some tips you have
to help others have deeper conversations?

00:24:18.954 --> 00:24:20.914
JJ: That is a perfect question to end on.

00:24:20.956 --> 00:24:23.208
I shuffled the deck
and it landed the right way.

00:24:23.208 --> 00:24:24.376
You're welcome.

00:24:24.418 --> 00:24:26.086
Tips to have deeper conversations?

00:24:26.712 --> 00:24:30.048
I would get really good
at asking questions.

00:24:30.048 --> 00:24:31.508
And I'll give you one example.

00:24:31.508 --> 00:24:33.802
I used to be in public radio,

00:24:33.844 --> 00:24:37.681
and when I started to get on the air
and people noticed me, they would --

00:24:37.723 --> 00:24:39.099
it happened over and over --

00:24:39.099 --> 00:24:40.559
"Oh, you're Joshua Johnson?

00:24:40.559 --> 00:24:43.061
Oh, I never would have
guessed you were Bla --

00:24:44.604 --> 00:24:46.982
Tall! I never would have thought
you were so tall.

00:24:47.024 --> 00:24:48.942
You sound three-foot-nine on the radio."

00:24:48.942 --> 00:24:51.528
Happened over and over
and it used to make me crazy.

00:24:51.528 --> 00:24:55.949
And I finally figured out
a simple two-word response

00:24:55.991 --> 00:24:57.367
that changed everything.

00:24:57.409 --> 00:24:58.577
Not those two words.

00:24:58.618 --> 00:25:00.370
When they would say,

00:25:00.412 --> 00:25:02.748
"I never would have guessed
you were Black."

00:25:02.748 --> 00:25:04.708
My response was,

00:25:04.750 --> 00:25:05.876
why not?

00:25:07.794 --> 00:25:10.464
And pause and don't say anything.

00:25:10.505 --> 00:25:13.508
BM: That's what one
of my tips is going to be,

00:25:13.508 --> 00:25:15.343
be very comfortable with silence.

00:25:15.385 --> 00:25:16.845
JJ: Well and here's the thing.

00:25:16.845 --> 00:25:19.723
What I finally learned was,
there's nothing I can say to you

00:25:19.723 --> 00:25:22.309
that will hurt more
than what you will say to yourself.

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:24.644
And when they reflect back to themselves,

00:25:24.644 --> 00:25:26.646
why they actually said that,

00:25:26.688 --> 00:25:28.482
they went, "Oh my God, I'm so sorry."

00:25:28.523 --> 00:25:31.026
And I say, "It's OK, but now you know,

00:25:31.026 --> 00:25:34.488
and now you can decide
if that's the person you want to be.

00:25:34.529 --> 00:25:36.573
But it's not my problem anymore.

00:25:36.615 --> 00:25:37.866
It's your problem."

00:25:37.866 --> 00:25:40.702
And that gave me peace, I could let it go,

00:25:40.702 --> 00:25:42.621
I didn't have to walk through life like,

00:25:42.662 --> 00:25:45.457
"I can't believe these white people,
the way they treat us,

00:25:45.457 --> 00:25:46.750
we ain't never gonna rise."

00:25:46.792 --> 00:25:47.959
And I didn't have to --

00:25:48.001 --> 00:25:50.212
babies don’t sleep that well.

00:25:50.712 --> 00:25:52.923
And I think the right question,

00:25:52.923 --> 00:25:57.803
plus the confidence
to stand on the question

00:25:57.803 --> 00:25:59.221
and just let it sit,

00:25:59.221 --> 00:26:00.806
whatever the response is.

00:26:01.181 --> 00:26:03.350
And so going through life
with a willingness

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:05.936
to ask more questions
than you make statements?

00:26:06.561 --> 00:26:08.021
Liberating.

00:26:08.021 --> 00:26:11.024
Nothing that happens after the question
is your responsibility.

00:26:11.024 --> 00:26:12.317
Your responsibility is [to]

00:26:12.317 --> 00:26:15.821
have the curiosity, the integrity,

00:26:15.821 --> 00:26:18.782
the perspicacity, the thoughtfulness,

00:26:18.824 --> 00:26:21.868
and the humility to ask
what's on your mind

00:26:21.868 --> 00:26:24.704
and not bite your tongue because,

00:26:24.704 --> 00:26:27.749
"Well, it might be uncomfortable,"
"What are they going to say?"

00:26:27.749 --> 00:26:29.042
Let that be their problem.

00:26:29.042 --> 00:26:30.335
I have a question for you.

00:26:30.335 --> 00:26:33.338
"Is it OK if I ask,
or did you just need to be heard?

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:34.965
Is it OK if I put this out there?

00:26:34.965 --> 00:26:36.758
Or is now not the good time?"

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.343
"OK, sure, ask."

00:26:38.343 --> 00:26:40.804
Say it and then stand on it.

00:26:40.804 --> 00:26:42.597
Makes life much, much easier.

00:26:42.597 --> 00:26:43.807
BM: OK, great.

00:26:44.182 --> 00:26:47.102
I think that deep
conversations require time.

00:26:48.186 --> 00:26:51.064
I think that if you really
want to have a deep conversation,

00:26:51.064 --> 00:26:56.403
you need to have undistracted time.

00:26:56.987 --> 00:26:59.489
And it's kind of a little bit
of what you said around

00:26:59.489 --> 00:27:03.368
ask the question and then
be comfortable being silent

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:09.583
because it's in the pause
where the depth can come out.

00:27:09.916 --> 00:27:13.545
And I think that so much
of our conversations with people,

00:27:13.545 --> 00:27:15.630
the reason why they're not deep

00:27:15.672 --> 00:27:18.133
is because we're always pressed for time

00:27:18.175 --> 00:27:20.719
and our attention is always scattered.

00:27:20.719 --> 00:27:23.180
And so in order to have
deep conversations,

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:26.641
that's why I love hosting
this podcast now,

00:27:26.641 --> 00:27:30.145
because I know that the person
has set aside two hours.

00:27:30.562 --> 00:27:33.398
And I might only have an hour's
worth of questions for them,

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:35.358
but I'm not in any rush, you know.

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:38.945
And so when I ask the question,
I have plenty of time

00:27:38.945 --> 00:27:40.488
to allow them to go deeper.

00:27:40.488 --> 00:27:43.283
And then often, also what you said I liked

00:27:43.325 --> 00:27:46.119
is like, asking a clarifying question

00:27:46.119 --> 00:27:47.621
around the first question.

00:27:47.996 --> 00:27:50.498
So not just jumping to the next question,

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:53.543
but then asking a question
that helps them go deeper.

00:27:54.377 --> 00:27:56.171
And then sitting and having the time.

00:27:56.213 --> 00:27:58.924
So I guess it really is about making sure

00:27:58.924 --> 00:28:01.468
that there's enough time
for the person to go deep.

00:28:01.509 --> 00:28:04.387
JJ: Blake, thank you, sir,
and thank you for this.

00:28:04.387 --> 00:28:06.431
This is fantastic. I really appreciate it.

00:28:06.431 --> 00:28:08.683
BM: Thank you so much, it was awesome.

